# umap_vs_tsne: three clusters in 4-D where two are close and
# one is far away. Both t-SNE and UMAP build a fuzzy-graph view
# of local neighborhoods; they differ in how they preserve the
# global distance between clusters.
#
# Legend mapping (class label -> color in the scatter):
#   0 = origin cluster (blue)
#   1 = near-origin cluster (pink)
#   2 = far-out cluster (green)
#
# Expectation: t-SNE tends to inflate every cluster to a similar
# size, washing out the "class 2 is much farther than 0 is from 1"
# global signal. UMAP preserves both the local neighborhood
# structure AND the relative inter-cluster distances -- the
# embedding shows class 2 visibly separated from {0, 1}.
pts_a = randn(1, [30, 4]) * 0.5 + matmul(ones([30, 1]), [[0, 0, 0, 0]])              # class 0 cluster at origin
pts_b = randn(2, [30, 4]) * 0.5 + matmul(ones([30, 1]), [[3, 0, 0, 0]])              # class 1 cluster near origin
pts_c = randn(3, [30, 4]) * 0.5 + matmul(ones([30, 1]), [[15, 0, 0, 0]])             # class 2 cluster 5x farther out
X = concat(concat(pts_a, pts_b, 0), pts_c, 0)                                       # 90 x 4
labels = concat(concat(zeros([30]), ones([30]), 0), ones([30]) + 1, 0)              # 30 zeros, 30 ones, 30 twos
# t-SNE: per-point perplexity-calibrated probabilities + KL on
# Student-t low-D affinity. Local structure stays sharp; global
# distance is not in the objective.
tsne_proj = tsne(X, 10, 200, 1)
# UMAP: per-point sigma-calibrated fuzzy memberships + cross-
# entropy with negative sampling. Both local AND global
# distances enter the objective via the repulsive force.
umap_proj = umap(X, 10, 0.1, 200, 1)
scatter_labeled(tsne_proj, labels)
scatter_labeled(umap_proj, labels)
